% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 12, section 12.2, Example 1 ("Beliefs for the trolley
%         problem") and Example 2 ("The current situation").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The book's beliefs are transcribed with generic variables. "No one saves the
% person" is modelled as negation as failure over an existential: "it is not the
% case that an agent saves the person ...". The book's "stops OR diverts" is
% split into two clauses for "saves". The key result: because john (stops the
% train) and mary (diverts it) both save the five people, "which person is
% destroyed" yields NO answers -- the naf condition fails. The original book
% template was "is killed"; it is renamed "is destroyed" here because "_ is
% killed" collides with LE's system template "_ is _".
%
% Note: "throws _ in front of _" has two readings (a person-throw, feeding
% "kills", and an object-throw, feeding "places"). The single fact "john throws
% boulder in front of train" matches both, so "john kills boulder" appears
% alongside the intended "villain kills victim". This is a faithful consequence
% of the book's overlapping vocabulary, not a modelling error.
%
% The book's optional rule "a train is speeding along a sidetrack if the train
% is speeding along a track and there is a sidetrack ahead and an agent pushes
% the button" is OMITTED: it introduces left recursion on "is speeding along"
% (and is not needed by the queries here).

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a person* is destroyed.
    *an agent* kills *a person*.
    *a person* is in danger of being killed by *a train*.
    *an agent* saves *a person* from being killed by *a train*.
    *an agent* throws *a person* in front of *a train*.
    *a person* is on *a railtrack*.
    *a train* is speeding along *a railtrack*.
    *a person* is unable to escape from *a railtrack*.
    *an agent* stops *a train*.
    *an agent* diverts *a train*.
    *an agent* places *a heavy object* in front of *a train*.
    *a heavy object* is next to *an agent*.
    *an agent* is within throwing distance of *an object* to *a railtrack*.
    *an agent* throws *an object* in front of *a train*.
    there is *a sidetrack* ahead of *a train*.
    *a second agent* is travelling on *a train*.
    *a second agent* pushes the sidetrack button.

the knowledge base trolley includes:

a person is destroyed
    if the person is in danger of being killed by a train
    and it is not the case that
        an agent saves the person from being killed by the train.

an agent kills a person
    if the agent throws the person in front of a train.

a person is in danger of being killed by a train
    if the person is on a railtrack
    and the train is speeding along the railtrack
    and the person is unable to escape from the railtrack.

an agent saves a person from being killed by a train
    if the agent stops the train.

an agent saves a person from being killed by a train
    if the agent diverts the train.

an agent stops a train
    if the agent places a heavy object in front of the train.

an agent places a heavy object in front of a train
    if the heavy object is next to the agent
    and the train is speeding along a railtrack
    and the agent is within throwing distance of the heavy object to the railtrack
    and the agent throws the heavy object in front of the train.

% Reconstruction: the book's rule reads "a train is diverted if there is a
% sidetrack ahead and someone on the train pushes the button"; the diverter is
% left implicit. To keep "saves" answers ground, we identify the diverting agent
% with the agent who pushes the sidetrack button.
an agent diverts a train
    if there is a sidetrack ahead of the train
    and the agent is travelling on the train
    and the agent pushes the sidetrack button.

scenario situation is:
    five_people is on maintrack.
    train is speeding along maintrack.
    five_people is unable to escape from maintrack.
    there is sidetrack ahead of train.
    mary is travelling on train.
    boulder is next to john.
    john is within throwing distance of boulder to maintrack.
    john throws boulder in front of train.
    mary pushes the sidetrack button.
    villain throws victim in front of train.
    danger expects answers ["five people is in danger of being killed by train"].
    saving expects answers [
        "john saves five people from being killed by train",
        "mary saves five people from being killed by train"
    ].
    destroyed expects answers [].
    killing expects answers [
        "john kills boulder",
        "villain kills victim"
    ].

query danger is:
    which person is in danger of being killed by which train.

query saving is:
    which agent saves five_people from being killed by which train.

query destroyed is:
    which person is destroyed.

query killing is:
    which agent kills which person.
